When using this approach :
- It is easy to get current sampled every few seconds
- Values perfectly map those obtained with the true RMS voltmeter on the leads coming from the SCT itself
- Measurements seem to be smoother using this approach than when using the ct_clamp component
- During idling (tested with the fridge circuit) there is slight current consumtion, confirmed by switching off circuit this to be noise
- "Noise" or lack of precision varies over time, and can be compensated for in the current calculations with the ESPhome sensor before sending to Home Assistant
- Power measured for the fridge is around 85 W whereas when using the ct_clamp approach it hovers around 100 W
- "Noise" may be partially due to the fact we were using a small amount of "accuracy_decimals", which would result in rounding (ADCs are 16 bit or a range of 65536 values across +- 2.04 V). For example, for a 15 A sensor, 6 mV turns out to be 20 W, but the calculation for RMS (0.006 * 0.006) results in 0,000036, which needs at least 6 decimals, using 5 would result in artifacts

It remains to be seen if by going this way for all circuits the measuring rig will continue working as good as in the case for a single circuit.

